<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Scythian Kingdom of Crimea</h1><p class="st-meta">250 BCE – 260 CE (approx.) · capital Scythian Neapolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last Scythian kingdom, ruled from Scythian Neapolis near Simferopol after Sarmatians drove the Scythians from the open steppe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As Sarmatian tribes overran the steppe in the 3rd century BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> who survived withdrew into the Crimean foothills and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. There they built a new state with a fortified royal seat, Scythian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neapolis">Neapolis</a>, on the edge of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simferopol">Simferopol</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> were more settled than their ancestors, farming, building stone houses and trading through the Greek cities of the coast. Their kings sought to control <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chersonesus-taurica">Chersonesos</a> and the grain trade. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skilurus">Skilurus</a> struck coins at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/olbia-pontica">Olbia</a>, and a stone mausoleum at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neapolis">Neapolis</a> holding dozens of burials and rich goods is often attributed to him.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palakus">Palakus</a> was defeated around 110 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diophantes">Diophantes</a>, general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a>, and the kingdom lost its leading role. It survived in a weaker form, often in conflict with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and other invaders destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neapolis">Neapolis</a> in the mid 3rd century CE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/scythian-kingdom-of-crimea.jpg" alt="Scythian Kingdom of Crimea" width="320" height="241"><figcaption>Image: Anonimu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scythia_Minor_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>250 BCE – 260 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Scythian Neapolis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Skilurus, Palakus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Scythian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Scythian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Late Scythian Kingdom, Lesser Scythia, Scythia Minor (Crimea)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=5&amp;focus=scythian-kingdom-of-crimea" aria-label="Scythian Kingdom of Crimea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5493 590 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Scythian Kingdom of Crimea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5813 846L5813 846M5813 846L5879 982M5813 846L5866 834"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5813 846h0M5879 982h0M5866 834h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5813 846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5813 846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Scythian Neapolis. <a href="/atlas?year=5&amp;focus=scythian-kingdom-of-crimea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
